"abs/yellowpaper/5730f4b6cf56fa88bb93bbda272dc449b2f77b17/decisions/v0.5.md\",\"status\":200,\"measuredAt\":\"2026-11-06T03:27:27.315Z\",\"body\":\"# FLOP Yellow Paper \u2014 Decision Record \u00b7 v0.5\\n\\nThis record accompanies the initial **v0.5.0 (draft)** public release. There has been no public\\nrelease of this paper yet; prepublication edits are folded into that baseline.\\n\\nThe [v0.4 decision record](v0.4.md) remains the immutable provenance for `D-0401`\u2013`D-0440`;\\n`D-0438`\u2013`D-0440` continue its genesis-sizing series and were recorded before publication.\\nThose decisions continue to apply except where a later decision explicitly supersedes their scope.\\nNew v0.5 decisions use `D-0501` onward. A decision's identifier is not the paper's patch version.\\nAfter a decision merges, revise it only through a new superseding ID.\\n\\n## Index\\n\\n| Decision | Scope | Status | Specification |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| [D-0501](#d-0501--public-claim-scope-and-reproducible-evidence) | Initial public claim scope, proof assumptions, evidence, and conformance | Draft for initial publication | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a70\u2013\u00a75, \u00a79\u2013\u00a715, Appendices E\u2013H |\\n| [D-0502](#d-0502--withdraw-unmeasured-capacity-and-latency-profile) | Capacity and latency claim disposition | Proposal \u2014 pending ratification | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a72.2, \u00a75.2\u2013\u00a75.3, \u00a712.1, E.46 |\\n| [D-0505](#d-0505--canonical-wire-and-accounting-profile) | Canonical encoding, rejection, replay domains, legacy cutoff, and `G_n` representation | Draft for initial publication | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a74, Appendices F\u2013H |\\n| [D-0507](#d-0507--post-testnet-genesis-validator-selection-and-placement-admission) | Genesis composition of the first active set, placement admission, per-domain cap | Proposal \u2014 pending ratification | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a715.2, \u00a715.5, E.41 |\\n| [D-0508](#d-0508--rotation-on-underperformance-floor-based-ejection-and-on-chain-performance-signal) | Floor-based ejection, on-chain performance signal, rotation throughput | Proposal \u2014 pending ratification | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a715.4, \u00a715.5 |\\n| [D-0509](#d-0509--committee-session-cadence-aligned-to-the-babe-epoch) | `AlephEpochBlocks` 900 \u2192 BABE epoch (3,600) | Proposal \u2014 pending ratification; consensus safety zone | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a72.2, \u00a715.4 |\\n| [D-0510](#d-0510--bootstrap-nodes-and-the-public-discovery-plane) | Bootnodes as a launch requirement: count, placement, node keys, chain-spec wiring, unique `protocol_id` | Proposal \u2014 pending ratification | bootnodes.md |\\n| [D-0506](#d-0506--paid-agent-memory-and-validator-data-services) | Paid encrypted application storage and retrieval, validator revenue, authority and liability | Draft; activation profile pending E.54 | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a75.4, Appendices D\u2013H |\\n| [D-0511](#d-0511--one-canonical-compute-channel-transcript-and-receipt-definition) | One canonical compute-channel transcript and receipt definition | Draft for initial publication | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a76.5, \u00a712.1, Appendix C.3\u2013C.4, Appendix F.3 |\\n| [D-0512](#d-0512--development-network-exception-to-the-paid-storage-activation-gate) | Paid storage on development networks ahead of E.54 | Ratified 2026-09-25 (owner); development networks only | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a75.4 R5.4k, R5.4q; Appendices E.54, H.3 |\\n| [D-0513](#d-0513--one-generic-ss58-prefix-on-every-flop-network) | Account-address prefix: generic Substrate `42` on every FLOP network; networks told apart by genesis hash | Ratified 2026-09-29 (owner) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a76.5 R6.5a, \u00a79.3 R9.8 |\\n| [D-0514](#d-0514--era-t-conversion-rules-and-a-fail-closed-miner-adjustment) | Era-T conversion: supply, eligibility and scoring inputs, snapshot of record; the miner airdrop adjustment fails closed on missing performance data | Ratified 2026-09-29 (owner) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a78.2 R8.3\u2013R8.5, R8.7; Appendices E.38, H.4 |\\n| [D-0515](#d-0515--airdrop-grant-landing-claims-and-locked-compute-spend) | Airdrop grants funded once as frozen genesis balances; finalized-head claims; locked principal spendable only on four compute calls | Ratified 2026-09-29 (owner) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a78.2 R8.6, R8.8; Appendix H.4 |\\n| [D-0517](#d-0517--airdrop-unlock-miner-earn-to-unlock-validator-halving-release) | Miner grant a quarter at genesis, the rest 1:1 against finalized inference settlement; Validator grant frozen to the first halving, then one per day; locked Miner principal usable only as self-stake, locked Validator principal immobile | Ratified 2026-09-30 (owner) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a78.2 R8.7, R8.8; Appendix E.38, H.4 |\\n| [D-0518](#d-0518--block-rewards-and-the-subsidy-are-liquid-on-issue) | Miner and validator block-reward legs and the Labs/Foundation subsidy liquid on issue, agent-leg payouts liquid once E.40 ratifies; validator earnings no longer auto-compound | Ratified 2026-09-30 (owner) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a79 R9.2, R9.13; Appendix D, E.40, H.4 |\\n| [D-0519](#d-0519--service-lane-close-boundary-claim-window-and-provider-only-redemption) | Paid-storage service lanes: close boundary, a claim window on the finalized prefix, bounded post-close exposure, provider-only redemption | Ratified 2026-10-04 (owner); activation profile pending E.54 | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a75.4 R5.4g, R5.4j; Appendices E.54, F.7, G.4 |\\n| [D-0521](#d-0521--usage-pins-protect-both-da-retention-classes) | Usage pins prevent pruning of Ephemeral and Leased blobs without extending expiry | Task #2228 implementation decision | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a75.3 R5.3d |\\n| [D-0522](#d-0522--agent-airdrop-spend-to-unlock) | Agent grant unlocks one FLOP per three FLOP of locked principal in settled `payable`s, spend capped at three quarters of principal, locked-first payout and refund; no end block | Ratified 2026-10-05 (owner); spend cap and attribution await owner confirmation in review | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a78.2 R8.7, R8.8; Appendices E.38, H.4 |\\n\\n| [D-0523](#d-0523--dormant-htlc-timeout-burn) | Default-zero HTLC burn implementation and proposed activation semantics | Proposal \u2014 pending ratification; not activated | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a710, E.48 |\\n\\n| [D-0524](#d-0524--slot-and-tranche-position-rent) | Rent rounding per slot and funding tranche, historical replacement entitlements and payer dust refunds | Ratified 2026-10-07 (owner); activation profile pending E.54 | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a75.4 R5.4e/f; Appendices E.54, F.7, G.4 |\\n| [D-0525](#d-0525--inference-fee-split-85-miner--15-validator) | Inference fee settlement split: 85% miner / 15% validator, 1% audit earmark inside the validator leg | Ratified 2026-10-14 (owner) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a79 R9.5, \u00a712.1 R12.1a/d; Appendices D, E.38, F.3, H.5 |\\n| [D-0526](#d-0526--active-set-cap-and-babe-authority-alignment-1000-active-validators-100-finalizers) | Active-set cap and BABE authority alignment (1,000 active validators, 100 finalizers) | Ratified 2026-10-15 (closes #1393 / YP E.41) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a72.2, \u00a715.2, \u00a715.4, \u00a715.5, E.41 |\\n| [D-0527](#d-0527--miner-airdrop-unlock-credits-miner-block-rewards) | Miner grant unlock credit counts the grantee's minted miner-leg block rewards, at finality, alongside its settled payouts; supersedes D-0517 item 1 | Ratified 2026-10-12 (owner; #2625) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a78.2 R8.7 |\\n| [D-0528](#d-0528--sponsor-owned-registration-bonds-count-as-self-stake-on-devnet-and-testnet) | Sponsor-owned, stake-only registration bonds count as operator self-stake on devnet and testnet only, refunded to the sponsor; mainnet unchanged | Ratified 2026-10-17 (owner; #2676, settles #683 for devnet/testnet) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a72.3, \u00a76.1 R6.1a, \u00a715.2; Appendices E.32, G.3, H.1 |\\n| [D-0529](#d-0529--zero-burn-across-all-protocol-paths) | Zero burn across all protocol paths: 0% transaction-fee burn (`BurnFees = 0`), 0% inference-fee burn, and 100% routing of slashes to `FLOPFoundationAccount` | Ratified 2026-09-22 (owner; #1945, #2792) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a79.1 R9.6, \u00a79.3 R9.10, \u00a711.3, \u00a713.2, \u00a715.7, Appendices A, D, H |\\n| [D-0530](#d-0530--operator-self-stake-only-and-duration-locked-community-staking-pool) | Prohibition of delegated staking into validators and miners (self-stake only); deprecation of delegation extrinsics and commissions; 5% duration-locked community staking pool | Ratified 2026-09-08 (owner; #2792) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a72.3, \u00a76.1, \u00a79.1 R9.12, \u00a711.3, \u00a715.2, \u00a715.5, Appendices A, C, D, E.32, E.40, G.3, H |\\n| [D-0531](#d-0531--off-chain-referral-leaderboard-and-lottery-program) | Off-chain explorer referral leaderboard and periodic referral lotteries funded from `genesis_reserve` (800M FLOP), superseding on-chain referral commissions | Ratified 2026-09-23 / 2026-09-24 (owner; #1358, #2792) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a79.1 R9.4, Appendices A, E.38 |\\n| [D-0532](#d-0532--miner-capacity-bond-one-unit-window-inside-the-coefficient-value-set-against-a-security-envelope) | Miner capacity bond: one unit (FLOP per G_n/s of calibrated cap), dispute window inside the coefficient, surge as a separate margin; `k` set at activation against a security envelope (0.25 candidate); amends D-0418 | Proposal \u2014 pending ratification (#1361) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a76.1, Appendices A, C.1, E.33, H |\\n| [D-0533](#d-0533--model-registry-base-price-per-g_n-a-reference-set-against-emission-at-a-reference-throughput) | Model-registry base price per `G_n`: a FLOP-denominated reference set by `\u03c6\u00b7P_m(era)/T_ref`, replacing the devnet-scale 0.003; 5e-7 FLOP is the benchmark to validate, and the value is set at activation alongside D-0532 | Proposal \u2014 pending ratification (#1361 follow-up) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a712.1, Appendices A, E.24, E.44, E.49 |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## D-0501 \u2014 Public claim scope and reproducible evidence\\n\\n**Status:** draft for initial public release. Defines the claim scope and evidence requirements of\\nv0.5.0; publication requires the prepublication evidence review. Runtime behavior, economic settings,\\nand cryptographic mechanisms are unchanged.\\n\\n**Context.** The first public paper needs a single, inspectable statement of its guarantees. Selection\\nstake and voting seats, artifact existence and proof applicability, signed work and verified execution,\\nand session call count and byte/work costs are separate quantities. Each claim needs explicit assumptions\\nand independently inspectable evidence. The finite-population counterexample and sizing calculations are reproduced in\\n[prepublication-evidence](../research/prepublication-evidence.md).\\n\\n**Decision.**\\n\\n1. **Consensus:** use AlephBFT's strict `< \u2153` Byzantine-seat premise (`3f < n`) throughout the current\\n   specification. The Lean `Consensus.Committee.quorum_inter_gt_third` and `Consensus.FinalCtx.nonforking`\\n   proofs apply with one unit of voting weight per seat, quorum-certified finalization, and honest\\n   non-equivocation. Selection stake and voting seats are different quantities. Withdraw any assertion that general\\n   unequal-stake sampling of distinct identities automatically satisfies a binomial tail bound at the\\n   attacker's aggregate stake fraction. The mechanism-specific capture proof remains an explicit open\\n   obligation; no secure global concentration threshold is inferred from this correction.\\n2. **Verification:** qualify D-0432's soundness wording. TEE independence is a target trust-domain\\n   requirement, not evidence that a complete SOFT settlement/dispute lane exists. The abstract fallback\\n   theorem is conditional on its supplied verifier-error bound and enforcement mapping. D-0431's\\n   mandatory commitment remains; presence, signature/aggregate consistency, execution checks, and\\n   reward eligibility are stated separately. A multiplicative error model requires justified\\n   independence or suitable conditional bounds. Closed-channel reward recovery remains open.\\n3. **Finality:** D-0430's freeze/recovery policy governs stalled finality. A best-head payout claim does\\n   not follow from a theorem about undisputed channels on one modeled chain. Remove conflicting\\n   normative permission for best-head escape; do not change the deployed finality path.\\n4. **Accounting and sizing:** qualify D-0405's measurement language as the implemented reference-work\\n   convention; physical counters, cache-aware work, and E.30's unit taxonomy require their own evidence\\n   or definition. Correct direct-rail quorum arithmetic; the inherited 125,000-byte propagation budget\\n   is a sizing assumption pending workload/topology measurements. D-0427's lane limits are admission targets;\\n   their lifecycle conversion excludes extra evidence calls and is not a measured throughput result.\\n5. **Conditional security:** economic deterrence requires effective collectible monitoring and bounded\\n   total exposure; it is not a Byzantine soundness theorem or a full equilibrium from one inequality.\\n   DA custody, HTLC inclusion, governance-approved runtime changes, and external demand each retain\\n   explicit assumptions and unresolved obligations. Historical design rationale is not proof of those\\n   assumptions.\\n6. **Publication:** every claim states whether its backing is mathematical, modeled, measured, or\\n   assumed. Export a curated reproducible evidence bundle and distinguish private artifact existence\\n   from independent reproducibility. Specification and runtime conformance statuses remain separate.\\n\\n**Alternatives.** Keeping the existing prose would retain false implications. Changing consensus,\\nsettlement economics, or the verifier while repairing publication would require separate protocol\\nchoices and implementation verification. This decision instead states the guarantees the current\\nmodels support and makes the remaining engineering/research obligations reviewable.\\n\\n**Consequences.** This narrows claims, not admission or payment gates. The public draft is suitable for\\nreview of specified mechanisms and assumptions; it does not certify production security, benchmarked\\ncapacity, or completion of Appendix E. The documented session-key lifetime is corrected from the\\nunchanged 864,000-block runtime constant, with a regression gate for its day conversion.\\n\\n**Supersedes by scope.** The unconditional security/measurement/sizing interpretations of D-0401,\\nD-0405, D-0427 and D-0432 described above; preserves their underlying protocol choices. Reaffirms\\nD-0430 for finality-sensitive settlement. No historical entry is rewritten.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## D-0502 \u2014 Withdraw unmeasured capacity and latency profile\\n\\n**Status:** proposal pending ratification. Claim-scope change only; runtime limits, dispatch, and\\nconsensus behavior are unchanged. This record does not ratify the disposition.\\n\\n**Mechanism status:** PENDING. E.46 stays open until runtime benchmarks and the disclosed network\\nmatrix exist; partial encoding evidence does not close it.\\n\\n**Context.** D-0427 proposed 96 steady/128 burst session-control transactions per block and derived\\n48/64 cooperative lifecycles per second from two inclusions. The paper also carried a 125,000-byte\\npropagation scenario and sub-second finality target. None was established by serialized runtime calls,\\nsupported runtime benchmarks, or an end-to-end network/workload matrix. Deterministic SCALE fixtures\\nnow include actual runtime pallet/call indices, extrinsic framing, signature and extension fields, DA\\nreferences, and boundary turn/path inputs. They show byte growth but cannot establish throughput or\\nlatency.\\n\\n**Proposed decision.** If ratified, remove all four figures from the v0.5 conformance profile. State only the configured 5\\nMiB block-length limit (75% Normal) and two-dimensional runtime weight envelope, neither as a capacity\\nguarantee. Publish charged dispatch weights as estimates, not measurements: the direct-attestation\\nweight identifies itself as an unbenchmarked stub, and session weights do not parameterize Merkle-path\\nlength or duplicate scanning. Keep E.46 open until the runtime-benchmark build succeeds and the\\ndisclosed network matrix reports successful, rejected, backlogged, and stalled runs. A future capacity\\nprofile requires a new decision and must distinguish chain admission, serving capacity, and\\nclient-perceived latency.\\n\\n**Evidence.** [Prepublication evidence](../research/prepublication-evidence.md) and its\\n[JSON](../evidence/yellowpaper-claim-evidence.json) / [CSV](../evidence/yellowpaper-claim-evidence.csv)\\nbundle. The recorded runtime-benchmark attempt remains blocked by #550: its WASM dependency graph\\nmixes Polkadot SDK generations, producing cross-version crypto/type API failures and incompatible\\n`sp-io` 38.0.2/48.0.0 implementations that abort on duplicate `panic_impl`. Therefore no runtime/build\\nhash or benchmark output exists. No network run was performed; the empty log/hash/hardware fields and\\nrequired workload matrix are retained as negative evidence.\\n\\n**Consequences if ratified.** The paper makes no finalized-lifecycle throughput or inclusion/finality percentile\\nclaim. Runtime admission continues to enforce its existing byte, weight, state, and validity gates.\\nMeasured profiles may be added only with raw logs, runtime/build hashes, generator and seeds, hardware\\nprofile, configuration, repetitions, uncertainty, and rerun instructions.\\n\\n**Would supersede by scope.** D-0427's throughput/admission figures and D-0501's temporary treatment of the\\n125,000-byte and latency figures as targets. Preserves D-0401's one-second BABE slot and every deployed\\nruntime limit.\\n\\n## D-0505 \u2014 Canonical wire and accounting profile\\n\\n**Status:** draft for initial public release; runtime and Rust/TypeScript/Python SDK changes are implemented\\nin the authoring repository. Publication remains subject to approval of the exact public bundle.\\n\\n**Context.** Appendix F previously summarized fields but left widths, container prefixes, version dispatch,\\ndomain separation, malformed input policy, tree behavior, and migration cutoffs implicit. The runtime tried\\nmultiple historical leaf hashes after signature failure, making the accepted format set difficult to audit.\\n`G_n` also lacked one numeric taxonomy across the direct and session rails.\\n\\n**Decision.** Adopt Appendix F's `flop-wire-v1` profile and its public byte-for-byte corpus.\\n\\n1. Fixed integers are unsigned little-endian; SCALE enums/options/bools and compact lengths are canonical\\n   and fail closed. Objects reject unknown tags, invalid presence values, truncation, overflow, overlong\\n   compact integers, and trailing bytes.\\n2. Direct-rail task producers use the versioned `FLOP/POUI/TASK` preimage. The runtime continues to receive\\n   its H256 as an opaque local replay key, so active validators must enforce that producer derivation before\\n   signing. Compute-channel IDs bind the genesis hash, parties, and nonce under a versioned protocol domain;\\n   receipts use their own domain/version and inherit deployment/session binding through the channel ID.\\n3. Every current settlement/dispute turn carries a `V0`\u2013`V3` SCALE tag. Verification selects exactly that\\n   preimage and never retries. Current policy-pinned channels accept V2/V3; pre-policy channels may explicitly\\n   select V0/V1. V2 is described as lower evidence and cannot be presented as V3/TOPLOC-bound assurance\\n   (#1404); policy-less legacy formats remain confined by the existing marker (#686).\\n4. Current DA transcripts use FCC4 with an explicit per-turn leaf version, strict optional-field consistency,\\n   and exact byte consumption. Historical unprefixed/FCC2/FCC3 reads are migration-only and produce one\\n   explicit in-memory version before verification.\\n5. New channels require the tagged receipt. A new `ChannelWireProfiles=1` marker is the cutoff: only absent\\n   pre-upgrade channels accept the historical untagged receipt, while unknown values fail closed. No channel\\n   storage value, amount, leaf preimage, or historical signature is rewritten.\\n6. Settled `G_n` is whole `floor(F_eff/10\u2079)`: `u64` on the direct rail and `u128` for sessions, with checked\\n   conversion/aggregation. Profile v1 bills the full prompt/context term even when a KV-cache prefix is reused.\\n   Any proof-bound cache discount requires a new meter/profile version (#1153), never reinterpretation.\\n\\n**Alternatives.** A leading byte inside every historical leaf would invalidate existing roots and signatures.\\nContinuing trial verification would preserve ambiguity. An additive wire-profile map distinguishes new opens\\nwithout rewriting in-flight channel storage.\\n\\n**Consequences.** E.30 closes; E.51 remains partial. The fixture publishes schema, inputs, exact\\nbytes/hashes/signature, positive and malformed/domain/version cases, generation commands, and consumer\\npaths. Production producer/validator enforcement of `task_hash` v1 and cross-version channel-resume receipt\\nselection remain open in E.51. V2's unbound TOPLOC field and proof-bound cache discounts remain separately\\nvisible under #1404 and #1153/E.22.\\n\\n**Supersedes by scope.** Appendix F's unversioned receipt and trial-dispatch descriptions, and D-0501's\\nplaceholder treatment of the `G_n` numeric taxonomy. Historical V0\u2013V3 hash preimages remain unchanged.\\n\\n## D-0506 \u2014 Paid agent memory and validator data services\\n\\n**Status:** draft for initial publication, implementing the requested paid-memory product direction.\\nProtocol profile and numerical settings remain subject to E.54 ratification and implementation;\\nthis decision does not activate a runtime fee.\\n\\n**Context.** D-0412 makes DA a validator duty with no byte-fee revenue. Its two retention classes cover\\nprotocol evidence and leased model images. Agents also need private, versioned application storage\\nand must pay the validators retaining and returning those bytes.\\n\\n**Decision.**\\n\\n1. Add a separate paid application-object namespace, retaining sovereign validator custody and\\n   FLOP-denominated transfers. Preserve existing protocol/model duties and `DataRef` v1 encoding.\\n   FLOP is not a storage chain: keep on chain only the epoch tariff and capacity declarations, one\\n   reservation per payer, service lanes, one checkpoint per provider per epoch, and disputes. Object\\n   commitments, manifests and ordinary memory heads live with the assigned provider group, sequenced\\n   by the generation-order sequencer and quorum-receipted; heads reach the chain only through\\n   checkpoint roots or an optional owner pin. Agents, sessions and sub-agents operate inside one\\n   payer's reservation through delegated capabilities anchored in agent-wallet limits.\\n2. Fund storage as a fixed-term capacity reservation of reserved encoded bytes on a protocol-assigned\\n   provider set, priced by one epoch tariff: a per-write setup fee plus rent on reserved byte-blocks.\\n   The tariff is the stake-weighted median of the votes of validators that declare capacity for the\\n   epoch, clamped to a per-epoch step bound and any governance floor/ceiling, and stepped up when\\n   declared capacity stays below demand headroom. Assignment is a stake-weighted random sample among\\n   declarers; the payer cannot choose providers. Release rent per epoch against accepted audit and\\n   checkpoint state, rounded once per provider; earn setup at each write's activation receipts;\\n   include audit/repair/checkpoint overhead and bound liability. A reservation is take-or-pay:\\n   deleting an object never refunds or settles anything, it frees the bytes for reuse within the same\\n   term; the remaining term is transferable to another payer by signed consent. Keep\\n   failed-activation/provider-fault remedies, unused lane budgets and refundable deposits separate.\\n   Repair cannot repeat setup. Voting is not proof of costs; voluntary capacity and the step rule are\\n   the clearing mechanism.\\n3. Pay setup and retrieval through one funded service lane per assigned provider, shared by every\\n   namespace and delegate, using payer-signed cumulative vouchers over activated writes, verified\\n   encoded bytes and requests at the lane's pinned tariff version. No log-based debits, double\\n   redemption, new issuance, inference reward credit, or automatic committee-duty credit. The request\\n   fee is advanced with the request, before the first chunk, so the unpaid first chunk is no longer\\n   a free good.\\n   Retrieval pricing controls demand for validator traffic; it does not prove traffic safety by itself.\\n   Require direct authenticated pull, bounded retries and atomic aggregate work/egress/credit budgets\\n   that survive lane churn and restart, including failed admission and repair. Protect protocol reserves;\\n   fees recycled within a payer/provider coalition are not an external attack cost. No automatic burn\\n   or new protocol take is introduced.\\n4. Require conforming clients to encrypt private objects; validators enforce the envelope, commitments,\\n   signatures and authority, without claiming to prove encryption, secret/random keys or plaintext\\n   absence for arbitrary uploaders. Authorize operations with attenuated capabilities and wallet limits.\\n   Use immutable versions and compare-and-swap heads with a provider-quorum receipt, equivocation as\\n   an objective slashable fault, and an optional chain-pinned head; distinguish revocation and\\n   tombstones from erasure of copies already acquired.\\n5. Require admitted capacity/collateral, independent placement, reconstruction at activation,\\n   objective post-commit audit proofs, bounded repair, and historical assignment liability after exit.\\n   Calibrate audit sampling so that audit probability times the cost of re-fetching a reconstructing\\n   shard set at the epoch tariff is at least the provider's rent share per epoch, under E.47's\\n   independence premise. Count accrued-but-unreleased rent as forfeitable bond. Existing\\n   known-answer hashes alone cannot establish continuous paid custody.\\n6. Keep the extension inactive until E.54 fixes the wire/security/economic profile and E.47's relevant\\n   placement and repair premises are demonstrated. Select rates and limits from measured costs and\\n   adversarial economic scenarios; do not invent a launch price or modify emission allocation.\\n\\n**Supersedes by scope.** D-0412's prohibition on DA-fee income applies to \u00a75.3 protocol/model duties;\\nit no longer prohibits the separately authorized paid application objects in \u00a75.4. D-0412 model\\nlease deposits and free top-ups, D-0407 lease lifecycles, and protocol-evidence access remain intact. For paid reservations only,\\nhistorical assignment liability replaces the \u00a75.3 live-set-only penalty rule; a replacement is never\\nslashed for its predecessor's service interval. Open PR #548's proposed model-storage economics are\\nseparate and are neither ratified nor activated by this decision.\\n\\n**Specification.** \u00a75.4, Appendices D, E.54, F.7, G.4, and H.3.\\n\\n**Rationale & sources.** Agent memory storage market;\\neconomics, mechanism and security review.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## D-0507 \u2014 Post-testnet genesis validator selection and placement admission\\n\\n**Status:** proposal, pending ratification. Spec-only; the wiring task is #1713. Depends on\\nE.41 (#1393) for any active set above the runtime's 200-authority bound.\\n\\n**Context.** D-0437 caps the active set at 1,000 and D-0401/\u00a715.4 sample a 100-seat committee from it,\\nbut nothing says how the first 1,000 are chosen. Today `register` enters `ValidatorQueue` only, and\\npromotion runs at [`validators_to_promote`](../yellowpaper.md#param-validators_to_promote) = 50 per\\n30-day rotation, so filling the set from the queue alone would take about 600 days. The earlier\\nallocation (\\\"800 guardian + 200 auction\\\", ECON-003 \\\"top 800 testnet validators\\\" at the legacy 305,505\\nbond) predates D-0421 (auction removed) and D-0440 (bond 1,200,000). Promotion is first-come at the\\nminimum stake (`queue.pop()`), the squatting case D-0437 flags. Placement is not an admission input\\nanywhere, although the seat-capture bound only holds when no fault domain exceeds about 10 % of the\\npool (validator-set-diversity \u00a73.3: the finite-pool profile\\nbounds a 10 % domain at \u2264 5.7e-8/yr and a 15 % domain at \u2264 1.3e-2/yr, while the assumption-free bound\\n\u2014 seats never exceed identities \u2014 protects only up to 33 identities, one fewer than the 34 seats\\nthat break `3f < n` at n = 100).\\n\\n**Decision.**\\n\\n1. **Genesis seeds the active set directly.** The `flop-mainnet` (and `flop-testnet`) genesis preset\\n   places the ranked cohort into `ActiveValidators` up to\\n   [`validator_active_set_cap`](../yellowpaper.md#param-validator_active_set_cap), with session keys and\\n   bonded self-stake set at genesis and an empty queue. Queue-and-rotation admission (\u00a715.5) applies only\\n   after genesis.\\n2. **Ranking key is earned work, not stake.** Testnet stake is faucet-issued, so the cohort is ranked by\\n   verification liveness on testnet \u2014 accepted verification duties per epoch (the `validatorEpochs`\\n   signal of the testnet earnings rules) \u2014 then uptime, then earlier registration as the tie-break.\\n   Stake enters only as the bond gate at genesis.\\n3. **Placement is an admission input, capped on count and on stake.** Each candidate signs a placement\\n   declaration (hosting ASN, metro, operating entity). Genesis composition rejects any cohort in which\\n   one hosting ASN, one metro, or one operating group exceeds **10 % of the seeded set by validator\\n   count or 10 % of seeded self-stake**, or in which one entity exceeds `MaxSlotsPerEntity`. Both legs\\n   are required because the committee draw is stake-weighted while the capture bound is modelled on a\\n   uniform draw over identities (E.42); capping count alone "
